Subject: [PATCH 15/15] LOCAL DIVERGENCE: make the Forth-source loader actually run

Two defects in libopenbios/initprogram.c that between them mean OpenBIOS's
Forth-source loader has never evaluated a byte -- on any arch, for as long as
these lines have existed. Both are silent.

DELIBERATELY NOT SENT UPSTREAM. This is a divergence we carry, and the reason is
a difference in goals rather than a judgement about the code: upstream maintains
a firmware that boots operating systems under QEMU, and nothing it ships needs
`load` of a .fth to work. This lab is a teaching artifact, and needs exactly that
path -- loading Forth off media is what makes multi-line test Forth possible at
all, given the firmware's ~80-char serial input truncation. Handing a maintainer
a fix for a road they do not drive on is a cost to them, not a gift.

Developed in a SEPARATE TREE (~/openbios-lab-archneutral/) because these are the
first arch-neutral lines this lab has touched -- the file is shared with x86, ppc
and sparc, and three targets this lab does not exercise should not change on the
strength of an amd64 measurement.

(a) TWO RECORDS OF ONE FACT. init_forth_context() evaluates `load-base` for
    `load-state >ls.file-size` bytes. The C loaders in libopenbios/load.c each
    set that field. But `load` does not go through them: $load
    (forth/debugging/client.fs:135) calls the DEVICE PACKAGE's own `load` method
    and records the size with `!load-size`, which writes a SEPARATE
    `variable file-size` (client.fs:37-41). Nothing connects the two.

    Measured: after `load /ide@1/cdrom@0:\marker.fth`, load-base held
    `\ marker.." SPIK` -- the file, intact -- while `load-state >ls.file-size @`
    printed 0 and `load-size` printed 23 (hex; the file is 35 bytes). So the
    evaluation ran over ZERO bytes and returned ok.

    Fixed as a fallback, not an overwrite: where a C loader has already filled
    the field in, that value is authoritative and is left alone.

(b) `eval2` DOES NOT EXIST. The `fword("eval2")` at the end of
    init_forth_context() was the ONLY occurrence of that name in the tree, and
    nothing defines it. fword() on a missing word is silent, which is why the
    symptom was "Evaluating Forth..." followed by a clean ok. Confirmed at the
    prompt: `load-base load-size eval2` answers `eval2: undefined word.`, while
    `load-base load-size evaluate` prints the marker. The real word is `evaluate`
    ( str len -- ), forth/bootstrap/interpreter.fs:163.

The size selection is done in C rather than in the feval string on purpose:
`if`/`then` are compile-only, so a fallback written as interpreted Forth does not
run -- and it fails exactly as silently as the bug it is fixing, which cost a
build to notice.

WHAT THIS BUYS. With patch 14 (amd64's binding fix) and this one:

    0 > load /ide@1/cdrom@0:\marker.fth
    0 > go
    switching to new context:
    Evaluating Forth...
    SPIKE-FORTH-LOADED
     ok
    0 >

`go` now works, not just the `load-base load-size evaluate` recipe patch 14
documented. That also RETIRES a fourth defect this lab had recorded as UNKNOWN
("with 2 and 3 fixed, the go trampoline still evaluates nothing"): it does not
exist. It was an artifact of a measurement taken before the eval2 change was in
the running image, and it is named here rather than quietly dropped.

NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT PER ARCH, and measured rather than assumed: x86
built with this patch and WITHOUT an x86 equivalent of patch 14 still ends `load`
in "Unable to locate (init-program)!", because that binding defect is per-arch.
Only amd64 carries the binding fix today.

x86 regression in the divergence tree: multiboot, dict-identity and nvram all
PASS. amd64: all five tracks PASS.

--- a/libopenbios/initprogram.c
+++ b/libopenbios/initprogram.c
@@ -114,9 +114,48 @@
 {
 	/* Execute Forth payload */
 	printk("Evaluating Forth...\n");
-	fword("load-base");
-	feval("load-state >ls.file-size @");
-	fword("eval2");
+
+	/* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). Two defects on this path, both silent, and
+	 * both of which mean the Forth-source loader has never run a byte.
+	 *
+	 * (a) TWO RECORDS OF ONE FACT. The C loaders in libopenbios/load.c each
+	 *     set `load-state >ls.file-size` themselves, which is what this
+	 *     function reads. But `load` does not go through them: $load
+	 *     (forth/debugging/client.fs:135) calls the DEVICE PACKAGE's own
+	 *     `load` method and records the result with `!load-size`, which
+	 *     writes a separate `variable file-size` (client.fs:37-41). Nothing
+	 *     connects the two, so the size read here is 0 and the evaluation
+	 *     covers no bytes. Measured: load-base held `\ marker.." SPIK` while
+	 *     `load-state >ls.file-size @` printed 0.
+	 *
+	 *     Fall back rather than overwrite -- where a C loader has already
+	 *     filled the field in, that value is authoritative and is left alone.
+	 *
+	 * (b) `eval2` IS NOT DEFINED ANYWHERE IN THE TREE. This call site was its
+	 *     only occurrence; nothing defines it, and fword() on a missing word
+	 *     is silent, so the symptom was "Evaluating Forth..." followed by a
+	 *     clean ok. The real word is `evaluate` ( str len -- ),
+	 *     forth/bootstrap/interpreter.fs:163.
+	 *
+	 * The size selection is done in C and not in the feval string on purpose:
+	 * `if`/`then` are compile-only, so a fallback written as interpreted Forth
+	 * does not run -- and it fails exactly as silently as the bug it fixes,
+	 * which cost a build to notice. */
+	{
+		ucell size;
+
+		feval("load-state >ls.file-size @");
+		size = POP();
+		if (size == 0) {
+			feval("load-size");
+			size = POP();
+		}
+
+		fword("load-base");
+		PUSH(size);
+	}
+
+	fword("evaluate");
 }
 
 void go(void)
